: Requirements and where the book teaches them {#tbl-crm-requirements}

## Design {#sec-crm-design}

### Responsibilities

Following the single-responsibility principle (@sec-solid) and the lesson of
Part I's project (@sec-project-design), the program is split into components
that each have one reason to change:




```{mermaid}
%%| echo: false
%%| fig-cap: "Architecture of the CRM. Arrows are dependencies; the graph is acyclic and the domain (Crm, Customer, Interaction) depends neither on the user interface nor on the file format."
flowchart LR
    MAIN["main.cpp<br/>(wiring)"] --> UI["ConsoleUi<br/>(menus, validated input)"]
    MAIN --> STORE["CsvStorage<br/>(file format, load/save)"]
    UI --> CRM["Crm<br/>(customers by id, search)"]
    UI --> STORE
    STORE --> CRM
    CRM --> CUST["Customer<br/>(personal data + interactions)"]
    CUST --> INT["Interaction<br/>(abstract)"]
    APP["Appointment"] --> INT
    CON["Contract"] --> INT
```




- **`Interaction`** (abstract), **`Appointment`**, **`Contract`** — what
  happened between the company and a customer (R7).
- **`Customer`** — personal data, validated, and the customer's interactions,
  which it **owns**.
- **`Crm`** — the collection: unique ids, lookup, search, sorted listing. No
  input or output.
- **`CsvStorage`** — the only component that knows the file format
  (information hiding, @sec-modularity).
- **`ConsoleUi`** — the only component that talks to the user; it reads from
  and writes to *any* pair of streams, so tests can drive it.
- **`main`** — creates the objects, loads, runs the interface, saves.

### The class hierarchy




```{mermaid}
%%| echo: false
%%| fig-cap: "UML class diagram of the domain. A Customer owns (filled diamond) any number of Interactions through std::unique_ptr; Appointment and Contract override the pure virtual functions of Interaction."
classDiagram
    class Crm {
      -customers_ : map~uint32, Customer~
      +add_customer(data) uint32
      +remove_customer(id) bool
      +find(id) Customer* or null
      +search(text) vector~const Customer*~
      +customers() vector~const Customer*~
    }
    class Customer {
      -id_ : uint32
      -data_ : CustomerData
      -interactions_ : vector~unique_ptr~Interaction~~
      +update(data)
      +add_interaction(i)
      +find_interactions(text)
      +validate(data)$ CustomerData
    }
    class Interaction {
      <<abstract>>
      -id_ : uint32
      -date_ : Date
      -notes_ : string
      +kind()* string
      +details()* string
      +matches(text) bool
      +to_record() Record
      #add_fields(record)*
    }
    class Appointment {
      -time_ : TimeOfDay
      -agent_ : string
      -place_ : string
    }
    class Contract {
      -policy_number_ : string
      -product_ : Product
      -premium_cents_ : int64
      -end_date_ : Date
    }
    Crm "1" *-- "many" Customer
    Customer "1" *-- "many" Interaction
    Interaction <|-- Appointment
    Interaction <|-- Contract
```




`Interaction` uses the **template method** pattern [@gamma1994]: the
non-virtual `to_record()` writes the fields common to all interactions and then
calls the pure virtual `add_fields()`, which each subclass implements for its
own fields. The rest of the program — listing, searching, saving — only uses
the base class interface, polymorphically (@sec-virtual).

### What is stored

The brief leaves the content of the records open; these are my assumptions,
the kind of questions one would confirm with the business:

| Entity | Fields | Rules (class invariants) |
|---|---|---|
| customer | id, first name, last name, phone, e-mail, city | names required; phone: digits, spaces, `+ - /`, at least 6 digits; e-mail: one `@` followed by a dot; no line breaks |
| appointment | id, date, time, agent, place, notes | valid date and time; agent required |
| contract | id, signing date, policy number, product (car, home, life, health, other), annual premium, end date, notes | policy number required and unique; premium $> 0$; end date after signing date |

: Data model {#tbl-crm-data}

Amounts are stored as **integer cents** (`std::int64_t`), never as `double`
(@sec-c-floats); dates are validated calendar dates in ISO 8601 form
(`YYYY-MM-DD`), which sorts correctly as text and as a `Date` object (the
defaulted `<=>` of @sec-operator-overloading compares year, month, day in that
order). Every class establishes its invariant in its constructor and throws
`std::invalid_argument` otherwise (@sec-ctors), so an invalid customer or
contract **cannot exist** in memory.

### Data structures and their costs {#sec-crm-structures}

Requirement R10 asks for a structure that also represents the **links** between
customers and interactions. The design:

- customers in a **`std::map<std::uint32_t, Customer>`** keyed by a unique id;
- each `Customer` owns its interactions in a
  **`std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Interaction>>`**, kept sorted by date;
- interaction ids are unique in the whole CRM.

The link customer → interactions is **ownership** (deleting a customer destroys
its interactions automatically, by RAII, @sec-raii); the link interaction →
customer exists only in the file, as the `customer_id` column. Ids, rather than
pointers or positions, are what the user types and what the files store: they
are stable across insertions and deletions (@sec-invalidation) and across
program runs. With $n$ customers, $k$ interactions for the customer at hand, and
names of bounded length:

| Operation | Cost | Why |
|---|---|---|
| find a customer by id (edit, delete, open interactions) | $O(\log n)$ | red–black tree (@thm-red-black-height) |
| add a customer | $O(\log n)$ | map insertion |
| delete a customer | $O(\log n + k)$ | map erase + destruction of its interactions |
| list customers sorted by name | $O(n \log n)$ | `std::sort` with a strict weak order (@thm-strict-weak) |
| search by name | $O(n)$ | substring test on every customer |
| add an interaction | $O(\log k + k)$ | binary search for the position + vector insertion |
| list / search interactions of a customer | $O(k)$ | linear scan |

: Costs of the CRM operations {#tbl-crm-costs}

For an agency with a few thousand customers every operation takes microseconds,
so — as in Part I (@sec-project-design) — the decisive arguments are
**clarity** and **stability of references**, not speed. An `std::unordered_map`
would make lookups $O(1)$ expected, but the ordered map also gives files and
listings a predictable order for free.

### File format and robustness

Two CSV files with a header line (@sec-csv-cpp): `customers.csv` and
`interactions.csv`. Interactions of every kind share one table with a fixed set
of columns; the columns that do not apply to a kind stay empty. This keeps the
file readable in a spreadsheet and lets new kinds add columns without breaking
old files. Loading follows the robust pattern of @sec-report-example:

- **no files** → first run, empty CRM (not an error);
- a line with the wrong number of fields or an invalid value → **skipped**,
  with a warning naming file and line on **standard error**; the rest is
  loaded;
- if anything was skipped, the original files are copied to `*.bak` before the
  first save, so a damaged file never causes silent data loss;
- saving is **atomic**: each file is written to a temporary file and then
  renamed over the old one (@sec-atomic-writes), so a crash during a save never
  leaves a half-written file;
- data are saved **after every change** and once more at exit.

On the user side (R11), every answer goes through `std::getline` and a
validating parser (@sec-cpp-input) in a loop that re-asks with an explanation;
choosing an option by id is preceded by a list that shows the ids; deletions
ask for confirmation; and **end of input** (Ctrl+D, Ctrl+Z, the end of a
script) is turned into an `InputClosed` exception that unwinds to the main loop,
which ends the program normally — after which `main` saves. Nothing can leave
the program half-way through an operation with unsaved or inconsistent data.

### Extensibility

The brief stresses that the prototype is the base of a product. Two examples
of changes and their cost in this design:

- **a new kind of interaction** (say, a *claim*): one new class derived from
  `Interaction`, one branch in `interaction_from_record`, its columns in the
  storage, and a menu entry. Listing, searching and deleting work unchanged —
  the open–closed principle (@sec-solid);
- **a different storage** (a database instead of CSV files): a new class with
  `load` and `save`; neither the domain nor the interface changes.

## Reference implementation {#sec-crm-reference}

The code is organised as in @sec-multifile: one header (the interface) and one
source file (the implementation) per component, in `namespace crm`. Each block
below is one file of
[`cpp/_crm`](https://github.com/matteo-zirilli/ai-engineering-handbook/tree/main/cpp/_crm).

### Interactions {.deep}

```{.cpp filename="include/crm/interaction.hpp"}
// interaction.hpp — what happens between the company and a customer.
// Interaction is an abstract base class; Appointment and Contract are the two
// concrete kinds. A new kind is added by deriving a new class and extending
// interaction_from_record(): no other part of the program changes.
#pragma once

#include <cstdint>
#include <map>
#include <memory>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

#include "crm/date.hpp"

namespace crm {

// One row of a data file: column name -> text.
using Record = std::map<std::string, std::string>;

enum class Product { Car, Home, Life, Health, Other };
std::string product_name(Product p);
std::optional<Product> parse_product(std::string_view text);

class Interaction {
public:
    virtual ~Interaction() = default;            // deleted through Interaction* (unique_ptr)

    std::uint32_t id() const { return id_; }
    const Date& date() const { return date_; }
    const std::string& notes() const { return notes_; }

    virtual std::string kind() const = 0;        // "appointment", "contract", ...
    virtual std::string details() const = 0;     // one line for the user
    virtual bool matches(std::string_view text) const;   // used by the search
    Record to_record() const;                    // for saving: common fields + add_fields()

protected:
    Interaction(std::uint32_t id, Date date, std::string notes);
    virtual void add_fields(Record& record) const = 0;

private:
    std::uint32_t id_;
    Date date_;
    std::string notes_;
};

class Appointment final : public Interaction {
public:
    Appointment(std::uint32_t id, Date date, TimeOfDay time, std::string agent,
                std::string place, std::string notes);
    std::string kind() const override { return "appointment"; }
    std::string details() const override;
    const std::string& agent() const { return agent_; }

private:
    void add_fields(Record& record) const override;
    TimeOfDay time_;
    std::string agent_;
    std::string place_;
};

class Contract final : public Interaction {
public:
    Contract(std::uint32_t id, Date signed_on, std::string policy_number, Product product,
             std::int64_t annual_premium_cents, Date end_date, std::string notes);
    std::string kind() const override { return "contract"; }
    std::string details() const override;
    const std::string& policy_number() const { return policy_number_; }
    std::int64_t annual_premium_cents() const { return premium_cents_; }

private:
    void add_fields(Record& record) const override;
    std::string policy_number_;
    Product product_;
    std::int64_t premium_cents_;
    Date end_date_;
};

// Rebuild an interaction from a saved record; throws std::invalid_argument if invalid.
std::unique_ptr<Interaction> interaction_from_record(const Record& record);

// Validation shared by all classes: throws std::invalid_argument with a clear message.
void check_text(const std::string& value, const char* field, bool required, std::size_t max_len = 120);

}  // namespace crm
```

```{.cpp filename="src/interaction.cpp"}
// interaction.cpp — appointments, contracts and their (de)serialisation.
#include "crm/interaction.hpp"

#include <stdexcept>

#include "crm/text.hpp"

namespace crm {

void check_text(const std::string& value, const char* field, bool required, std::size_t max_len) {
    if (required && trim(value).empty())
        throw std::invalid_argument(std::string(field) + " must not be empty");
    if (value.size() > max_len)
        throw std::invalid_argument(std::string(field) + " is too long (max " + std::to_string(max_len) + " characters)");
    if (has_control_chars(value))
        throw std::invalid_argument(std::string(field) + " must not contain line breaks or control characters");
}

std::string product_name(Product p) {
    switch (p) {
    case Product::Car:    return "car";
    case Product::Home:   return "home";
    case Product::Life:   return "life";
    case Product::Health: return "health";
    case Product::Other:  return "other";
    }
    return "other";                              // unreachable; keeps compilers quiet
}

std::optional<Product> parse_product(std::string_view text) {
    const std::string t = to_lower(trim(text));
    for (Product p : {Product::Car, Product::Home, Product::Life, Product::Health, Product::Other})
        if (t == product_name(p)) return p;
    return std::nullopt;
}

// ---- Interaction ------------------------------------------------------------------------
Interaction::Interaction(std::uint32_t id, Date date, std::string notes)
    : id_(id), date_(date), notes_(trim(notes)) {
    check_text(notes_, "notes", false, 300);
}

bool Interaction::matches(std::string_view text) const {
    return contains_ci(kind(), text) || contains_ci(details(), text) || contains_ci(notes_, text);
}

Record Interaction::to_record() const {
    Record r{{"id", std::to_string(id_)}, {"kind", kind()}, {"date", date_.str()}, {"notes", notes_}};
    add_fields(r);                               // the derived class adds its own columns
    return r;
}

// ---- Appointment ------------------------------------------------------------------------
Appointment::Appointment(std::uint32_t id, Date date, TimeOfDay time, std::string agent,
                         std::string place, std::string notes)
    : Interaction(id, date, std::move(notes)), time_(time), agent_(trim(agent)), place_(trim(place)) {
    check_text(agent_, "agent", true);
    check_text(place_, "place", false);
}

std::string Appointment::details() const {
    return date().str() + " " + time_.str() + ", agent " + agent_ + (place_.empty() ? "" : ", at " + place_);
}

void Appointment::add_fields(Record& r) const {
    r["time"] = time_.str();
    r["agent"] = agent_;
    r["place"] = place_;
}

// ---- Contract ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Contract::Contract(std::uint32_t id, Date signed_on, std::string policy_number, Product product,
                   std::int64_t annual_premium_cents, Date end_date, std::string notes)
    : Interaction(id, signed_on, std::move(notes)), policy_number_(trim(policy_number)),
      product_(product), premium_cents_(annual_premium_cents), end_date_(end_date) {
    check_text(policy_number_, "policy number", true, 40);
    if (premium_cents_ <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument("the annual premium must be positive");
    if (end_date_ <= date()) throw std::invalid_argument("the end date must follow the signing date");
}

std::string Contract::details() const {
    return "policy " + policy_number_ + " (" + product_name(product_) + "), signed " + date().str() +
           ", premium " + format_money(premium_cents_) + " EUR/year, until " + end_date_.str();
}

void Contract::add_fields(Record& r) const {
    r["policy_number"] = policy_number_;
    r["product"] = product_name(product_);
    r["premium_cents"] = std::to_string(premium_cents_);
    r["end_date"] = end_date_.str();
}

// ---- factory ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
namespace {
const std::string& field(const Record& r, const std::string& name) {
    static const std::string empty;
    const auto it = r.find(name);
    return it == r.end() ? empty : it->second;
}
Date date_field(const Record& r, const std::string& name) {
    const auto d = Date::parse(field(r, name));
    if (!d) throw std::invalid_argument("invalid " + name + " '" + field(r, name) + "'");
    return *d;
}
}  // namespace

std::unique_ptr<Interaction> interaction_from_record(const Record& r) {
    const auto id = parse_int(field(r, "id"), 1, UINT32_MAX);
    if (!id) throw std::invalid_argument("invalid id '" + field(r, "id") + "'");
    const auto uid = static_cast<std::uint32_t>(*id);
    const std::string kind = field(r, "kind");
    if (kind == "appointment") {
        const auto time = TimeOfDay::parse(field(r, "time"));
        if (!time) throw std::invalid_argument("invalid time '" + field(r, "time") + "'");
        return std::make_unique<Appointment>(uid, date_field(r, "date"), *time, field(r, "agent"),
                                             field(r, "place"), field(r, "notes"));
    }
    if (kind == "contract") {
        const auto product = parse_product(field(r, "product"));
        const auto premium = parse_int(field(r, "premium_cents"), 1, INT64_MAX);
        if (!product) throw std::invalid_argument("invalid product '" + field(r, "product") + "'");
        if (!premium) throw std::invalid_argument("invalid premium '" + field(r, "premium_cents") + "'");
        return std::make_unique<Contract>(uid, date_field(r, "date"), field(r, "policy_number"), *product,
                                          *premium, date_field(r, "end_date"), field(r, "notes"));
    }
    throw std::invalid_argument("unknown interaction kind '" + kind + "'");
}

}  // namespace crm
```



### Customers and the CRM {.deep}

```{.cpp filename="include/crm/customer.hpp"}
// customer.hpp — a customer and the interactions it owns.
#pragma once

#include <cstdint>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>
#include <vector>

#include "crm/interaction.hpp"

namespace crm {

// The editable personal data of a customer (a plain struct: no invariant of its own).
struct CustomerData {
    std::string first_name;
    std::string last_name;
    std::string phone;      // optional
    std::string email;      // optional
    std::string city;       // optional
};

class Customer {
public:
    // Throws std::invalid_argument if the data are not valid (see validate()).
    Customer(std::uint32_t id, CustomerData data);

    // Throws std::invalid_argument with a message for the user if the data are invalid.
    static CustomerData validate(CustomerData data);

    std::uint32_t id() const { return id_; }
    const CustomerData& data() const { return data_; }
    std::string full_name() const { return data_.first_name + ' ' + data_.last_name; }

    // Replace the personal data; on invalid data, throws and leaves the customer unchanged.
    void update(CustomerData data);

    const std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Interaction>>& interactions() const { return interactions_; }
    void add_interaction(std::unique_ptr<Interaction> interaction);    // kept sorted by date
    bool remove_interaction(std::uint32_t interaction_id);
    std::vector<const Interaction*> find_interactions(std::string_view text) const;

private:
    std::uint32_t id_;
    CustomerData data_;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Interaction>> interactions_;   // owned: destroyed with the customer
};

}  // namespace crm
```

```{.cpp filename="src/customer.cpp"}
// customer.cpp — validation of personal data and management of interactions.
#include "crm/customer.hpp"

#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>

#include "crm/text.hpp"

namespace crm {

CustomerData Customer::validate(CustomerData d) {
    for (std::string* s : {&d.first_name, &d.last_name, &d.phone, &d.email, &d.city}) *s = trim(*s);
    check_text(d.first_name, "first name", true, 60);
    check_text(d.last_name, "last name", true, 60);
    check_text(d.phone, "phone", false, 30);
    check_text(d.email, "e-mail", false, 100);
    check_text(d.city, "city", false, 60);
    if (!d.phone.empty()) {
        int digits = 0;
        for (char c : d.phone) {
            if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') ++digits;
            else if (c != '+' && c != ' ' && c != '-' && c != '/')
                throw std::invalid_argument("the phone number may contain only digits, spaces, +, - and /");
        }
        if (digits < 6) throw std::invalid_argument("the phone number needs at least 6 digits");
    }
    if (!d.email.empty()) {                       // deliberately simple: one '@', a dot after it, no spaces
        const auto at = d.email.find('@');
        if (at == std::string::npos || at == 0 || d.email.find('@', at + 1) != std::string::npos ||
            d.email.find('.', at) == std::string::npos || d.email.back() == '.' ||
            d.email.find(' ') != std::string::npos)
            throw std::invalid_argument("the e-mail address is not valid");
    }
    return d;
}

Customer::Customer(std::uint32_t id, CustomerData data) : id_(id), data_(validate(std::move(data))) {}

void Customer::update(CustomerData data) {
    data_ = validate(std::move(data));            // validate first: strong exception guarantee
}

void Customer::add_interaction(std::unique_ptr<Interaction> interaction) {
    if (!interaction) throw std::invalid_argument("null interaction");
    // Insert keeping the vector sorted by (date, id): binary search for the position.
    const auto pos = std::upper_bound(
        interactions_.begin(), interactions_.end(), interaction,
        [](const auto& a, const auto& b) {
            return a->date() != b->date() ? a->date() < b->date() : a->id() < b->id();
        });
    interactions_.insert(pos, std::move(interaction));
}

bool Customer::remove_interaction(std::uint32_t interaction_id) {
    const auto it = std::find_if(interactions_.begin(), interactions_.end(),
                                 [&](const auto& i) { return i->id() == interaction_id; });
    if (it == interactions_.end()) return false;
    interactions_.erase(it);                      // unique_ptr deletes the object
    return true;
}

std::vector<const Interaction*> Customer::find_interactions(std::string_view text) const {
    std::vector<const Interaction*> found;
    for (const auto& i : interactions_)
        if (i->matches(text)) found.push_back(i.get());
    return found;
}

}  // namespace crm
